    {
      "block_id": 10,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "1.3.3 Vector Base Register (VBR) \n",
      "bbox": [
        69.0,
        419.3580017089844,
        291.5159912109375,
        438.6360168457031
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.11274509803921569,
        0.5294924264002328,
        0.4763333189721201,
        0.5538333546031605
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 11,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "The VBR contains the base address of the exception vector table in memory. The\ndisplacement of an exception vector adds to the value in this register, which accesses the\nvector table. \n",
      "bbox": [
        69.0,
        448.10400390625,
        549.1199340820312,
        492.6000061035156
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.11274509803921569,
        0.5657878837200127,
        0.8972547942516851,
        0.6219697046761561
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 12,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "1.3.4 Alternate Function Code Registers (SFC and DFC) \n",
      "bbox": [
        69.0,
        501.3580017089844,
        440.83990478515625,
        520.635986328125
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.11274509803921569,
        0.6330277799355863,
        0.7203266418058109,
        0.6573686696062184
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 13,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "The alternate function code registers contain 3-bit function codes. Function codes can be\nconsidered extensions of the 32-bit logical address that optionally provides as many as eight\n4-Gbyte address spaces. The processor automatically generates function codes to select\naddress spaces for data and programs at the user and supervisor modes. Certain\ninstructions use SFC and DFC to specify the function codes for operations. \n",
      "bbox": [
        69.0,
        530.10400390625,
        549.35986328125,
        602.6080322265625
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.11274509803921569,
        0.6693232372553661,
        0.8976468354268791,
        0.760868727558791
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 14,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "1.3.5 Acu Status Register (MC68EC030 only) \n",
      "bbox": [
        69.0,
        611.3570556640625,
        366.9759521484375,
        630.635009765625
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.11274509803921569,
        0.7719154743233112,
        0.5996339087392769,
        0.7962563254616477
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 15,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "The access control unit status register (ACUSR) is a 16-bit register containing the status\ninformation returned by execution of the PTEST instruction. The PTEST instruction\nsearches the access control (AC) registers to determine a match for a speciﬁed address. A\nmatch in either or both of the AC registers sets bit 6 in the ACUSR. All other bits in the\nACUSR are undeﬁned and must not be used. \n",
      "bbox": [
        69.0,
        640.10400390625,
        549.2039184570312,
        712.6080322265625
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.11274509803921569,
        0.8082121261442551,
        0.8973920236226001,
        0.8997576164476799
      ]
